WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.637 --> 00:00:04.717
- Some people who come to this house,

00:00:04.717 --> 00:00:08.381
it looks like something out of
a romantic magical fairytale.

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:12.548
But this romantic magical
fairytale, really is our home.

00:00:15.991 --> 00:00:17.453
- I'm Jasmine.

00:00:17.453 --> 00:00:18.466
- I'm Simon.

00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:22.633
- And we live out in the
Preseli  Hills in West Wales.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.053
We're part of a wide community

00:00:25.053 --> 00:00:27.112
that spreads over the whole
region in the local area

00:00:27.112 --> 00:00:30.195
of people learning to fence off here.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.704
When we first settled
down, made permanent home,

00:00:33.704 --> 00:00:36.058
it seemed fairly clear
to us, we much preferred

00:00:36.058 --> 00:00:38.402
to be in the countryside and I did like

00:00:38.402 --> 00:00:40.344
the sort of magical element of you know,

00:00:40.344 --> 00:00:44.034
the idea of fairies and
myths and stuff like that.

00:00:44.034 --> 00:00:46.653
- We built a small, simple, modest house

00:00:46.653 --> 00:00:49.656
out of the things that were around us,

00:00:49.656 --> 00:00:51.533
out of the things that we could easily get

00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:53.872
from waste and from the natural world

00:00:53.872 --> 00:00:56.633
rather than going and buying things.

00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:58.333
This is where we store our firewood to

00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.715
keep us warm through the winter.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:02.713
It took us about two months,
so we've used stones,

00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:06.355
mud, clay, wood, we brought
in some straw bales,

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:08.755
used quite a lot of those,
and it cost us around

00:01:08.755 --> 00:01:11.371
about two or three thousand pounds.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.656
- And the big house,
Simon was finishing it

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:15.553
to a very high standard, so it'll have

00:01:15.553 --> 00:01:19.720
a lot of longevity, it'll
last a long time, generations.

00:01:20.587 --> 00:01:23.811
And we only use recycled
materials, natural materials

00:01:23.811 --> 00:01:25.651
or things that we've grown available

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:27.192
from this piece of land.

00:01:27.192 --> 00:01:29.232
This is my greenhouse.

00:01:29.232 --> 00:01:32.077
You put the food in
relationship to the house

00:01:32.077 --> 00:01:33.875
and to the water and to the landscape,

00:01:33.875 --> 00:01:36.237
and instantly, in comes the fellows,

00:01:36.237 --> 00:01:37.690
the birds and the insects.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.592
- Our dirty water can feed the plants,

00:01:39.592 --> 00:01:42.523
our need for shelter,
can give nice shelter

00:01:42.523 --> 00:01:44.419
for other animals to come and live

00:01:44.419 --> 00:01:47.752
and we really can live in a way which is,

00:01:47.752 --> 00:01:52.391
sympathetic to the rest of
what's going on in nature.

00:01:52.391 --> 00:01:54.733
This is my workshop.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:57.853
My commute to work goes a bit like this,

00:01:57.853 --> 00:02:00.594
out of the front door,
dodging between the flowers

00:02:00.594 --> 00:02:02.898
and maybe picking a few
berries off the bush

00:02:02.898 --> 00:02:04.855
on the way past for a little snack

00:02:04.855 --> 00:02:07.105
until I get to my workshop.

00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:11.189
At the end of every day, I can sit back

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:13.635
and see the things that we've made

00:02:13.635 --> 00:02:15.509
and know where the piece
of wood had come from.

00:02:15.509 --> 00:02:17.411
The first night in the
house that you've made

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:20.589
with your own hands in exactly the way

00:02:20.589 --> 00:02:24.771
that you want it to be, is
an absolutely magical one.

00:02:24.771 --> 00:02:27.354
(gentle music)

